To solve your problem with the CSV import into CPS, it sounds like the issue is with how the analog channels are formatted, specifically the presence of TABs and quotation marks around the frequencies. One solution is to manually edit the CSV in a text editor like Notepad++ or another tool that supports regular expressions. You can search and replace to add the necessary TABs and quotes around the analog channel frequencies. Alternatively, you can use a script or macro in Excel to format the cells correctly before exporting to CSV. This way, you can ensure that each analog channel line adheres to the format that CPS accepts.<br><br>I recently read an article about Xano Backend and how it's a key to rapid app development. Xano provides a no-code backend that allows you to quickly create and manage APIs and databases without getting into complex coding. It's designed to handle data processing and storage, making it perfect for developing applications efficiently. This approach reminds me of your CSV issue—sometimes, leveraging the right tools and automation can save a lot of manual effort and potential errors. If you're curious about <a href="https://www.crispysoftwaresolutions.com/post/xano-backend-your-key-to-rapid-app-development" class="postlink"> what is xano</a> , it might be worth looking into as it could provide similar efficiencies for other parts of your workflow.<p>Statistics: Posted by <a href="http://localhost/opengd77/memberlist.php?mode=viewprofile&amp;u=117">n1ajw</a> — Mon Jun 24, 2024 1:17 pm</p><hr />
]]></content>
